routes.brief:
Coalesce with the candidate proposed by PAPER-LI-23: same id, parent, title and area. The first prerequisite is Gross–Zagier formulas and arithmetic heights (GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights); do not re-plan GZ.0–GZ.9. This paper is the foundation that PAPER-LI-23 extends to big CM points, so its layers come first in the Part II.

Layers, from Bruinier–Ehlen–Yang, Invent. Math. 225 (2021) (published numbering, appendix = §9):
1. Operators:
   - raising/lowering identities (Lemmas 3.1–3.2);
   - Rankin–Cohen brackets (Lemma 3.3, Proposition 3.6);
   - raising of Hejhal–Poincaré series (Proposition 3.4, Corollary 3.5);
   - sublattice restriction (Lemma 3.7).
2. Automorphic Green functions Φ_{m,μ}(z, h, s) on GSpin(n, 2) Shimura varieties:
   - explicit series, singularities and current equation (Proposition 4.1, Corollary 4.2, Theorem 4.3, Oda–Tsuzuki);
   - L² characterization (Theorem 4.4, Proposition 4.5);
   - higher Green functions as lifts of iterated raises (Proposition 4.7).
   Import the s = s_0 Green functions from GSpinSpecialDivisorHeights rather than building them twice.
3. CM values:
   - small CM cycles, Lemma 5.1, Corollary 5.2 and the convolution L-function (Lemma 5.3);
   - the average formula (Theorem 5.4) and the individual formula (Theorem 5.5, Corollary 5.6);
   - big CM cycles for real quadratic F with the Cohen operator (Propositions 5.7–5.8, Corollary 5.9, Theorem 5.10).
4. Appendix: Lemmas 9.1–9.3; see-saw and Borcherds products (Proposition 9.6, Corollary 9.7); Theorem 3.9/9.4.
5. Applications:
   - the resolvent kernel and Theorem 6.1;
   - signature (1, 2) (Proposition 6.2, Lemma 6.3 with √M restored per E1, Theorem 6.4);
   - higher Heegner cycles (Zhang, Xue), Theorem 6.5 and Corollary 6.6 (the finite part comes from GZ.7);
   - Proposition 6.7, Lemma 6.8 (fix the recursion per E2), Lemmas 6.14–6.15, Proposition 6.16, Theorem 6.12, Corollary 6.13.
6. Partial averages:
   - twisted divisors ((7.6) with χ_Δ per E6), twisted theta functions, Lemma 7.8;
   - the Millson lift (Theorem 7.9, constant 4√m per E3);
   - Zagier lifts (Theorem 7.10), Theorem 7.11 and Theorem 7.13 with the odd-j constant 2^{j−2} (E3);
   - Corollary 7.15, Lemmas 7.16–7.17, and Corollary 7.18 with the hypothesis (z, z′) ∉ Z^j(f) and 2^{s−1} genus characters (E4–E5).
7. Tests: the Examples of §8, especially G_2((1+√−3)/2, (1+√−7)/2) = −(3/√21)log|(32+7√21)^4/25|, which pins down the odd-j constant.

Imports, never re-planned:
- the Weil representation and theta kernels: MP.4–MP.5;
- binary Siegel–Weil: MP.6;
- the Shimura correspondence: MP.7;
- harmonic Maass forms and Jacobi forms: QM.3, QM.1;
- the incoherent Eisenstein series and its derivative: GZ.6;
- Heegner divisors over Z and finite intersections: GZ.7;
- genus characters: GN.2;
- CM points and ring class fields: HE.1;
- canonical models and Shimura reciprocity: ShimuraVarieties V4, V6;
- Kuga–Sato varieties: GeneralizedHeegnerCycles GH.0.
